 or social innovation  and entrepreneurship  resources on campus so i'm curious if  any of you  were active with the center for social  innovation or the center for  entrepreneurial studies or maybe  participated in some of the activities  that they sponsored that you could speak  to  i was very involved with the social  innovation club and  definitely uh they have many courses  but uh even like the regular business  courses allowed you to  apply the the ideas to a social problem  so i took a startup garage  the two quarters and you we we have many  teammates  actually trying to create ideas based on  social problems  and also the social innovation club is a  very active club  they meet frequently they bring great  speakers  and actually if you have a great idea  and you're ready to  to kick it off they have a price that i  believe i don't know if they have  increased the amount it's about  150 k usd if you want to start your  your own idea one student each year gets  it  so like there are many many resources  to to support the the social aspect  and even if you're working on profit i  also would like to share this  if you go to the gsb and if you have  loans and if you  go into the nonprofit sector the gsb  helps you pay your loans so there is no  excuse for you to say that you're not  going to the non-profit world because  you have a loan the gsb is ready to  support you and  as this there are many resources this  summer actually i did education pioneers  and since i was making less than my  classmates  the gsb gave me a stipend uh  to be able to do education pioneers so  like there are many many  resources so there is the is definitely  a place to go if you want to do social  entrepreneurship or social innovation  thank you so much does anyone have  anything else to add that was pretty  comprehensive you took full advantage  while you were here  excellent uh well maybe for ita and  david then  uh curious if you could each share a  favorite  academic experience maybe it was a  professor or a class itself or a class  project  something that you still reflect on and  remember fondly a few years out  yeah no i'm i'm happy to start so um  well i actually have three that i'll  share i'm sorry there were actually so  many things but i think they illustrate  like different  components of the experience so so and  and  you know what i'm about to say it's  going to have something to do with  climate but stanford climate ventures is  this incredible interdisciplinary cost  it's kind of  um there's like stanford startup garage  and there's a and there's a couple sort  of stanford  jsp courses that are like a startup  incubator but packaged as the course  and then there's a few variants on that  that are specific to certain like areas  of application  and so stanford climate ventures is kind  of the climate version of that but it's  taught by um  this great guy dave danielson who's  actually managing director at like bill  gates's you know multi-billion dollar  energy fund which is quite an  exceptional thing he's teaching like a  25 person class and you get to talk to  him a couple times a week  um and that was just incredible  experience he brings in just so many  industry lumiere so many interesting  people and like just the  connection like the connections that  have like engendered through that class  but also just  the experience of like working through  building a business and co-creating that  with these sort of industry experts was  was so  unique um so i was that was a favorite  one i encourage anyone who like finds a  way to gsb  to take one of these courses where i  didn't wind up becoming an entrepreneur  at least not  yet and but i like just having that  experience at stanford is is really  terrific to like meet investors and  practice pitching and  and sort of ideating on something um the  second  class my favorite of like this there's  many great sort of soft skills classes  at gsb but i loved art of self coaching  with 