ybe if i don't use it every day you  know do i think about it once a week  yeah actually and that's pretty pretty  powerful you'll hear this from a lot of  people  touchy feely a phenomenal course that  isn't just about what you say in  business environments but how you say it  how you connect with  others both uh professionally but also  personally in your personal  relationships which has been i think  pretty powerful  and then there's the fun classes uh i  got to take a class  with tyra banks all about personal brand  it was incredible we did a tv segment  with tyra we did  we went shopping we did all these things  um  it took a wine class you know there's  some fun classes  that have real lessons in them again do  i think about those  every day of my job no but i do think  about them  fairly frequently because they have  really tangible things you can take away  from them  and it's also an incredible lifetime  experience as well  i don't want you to feel left out i'm  having such fun hearing these stories  are there any favorite classes or  academic experiences for you  definitely one that i have applied the  most right now is managing growing  enterprises  it's a class based just on cases of  things that you never thought that you  will be leading  like breakouts between your board and  the co-founders  or like facing the pr like a press  problem and like out of the 15 cases  that i  read there have been three to five cases  in teach for mexico already that i have  faced  and i have actually i'm a holder of my  my cases so i still have those cases and  i go back to see  what what are the best things to do and  definitely is a class that i  that i recommend i i didn't realize that  it was going to be my favorite  until i started working at pitch for  mexico and i was like oh my god  let me read that case because i'm living  through that right now  amazing so jennifer two quick questions  for you  one wondering if you have the answer to  what eda mentioned do we  know uh how many independent studies a  student can pursue during their time in  the program  yes um so it's a unit limitation you can  count  four units of independent study towards  your mba so  what that means is um you could do  one unit four times so for a year  one unit or you could do them all at  once it just depends on how in-depth  your project is going to be  as far as how long you want it to take  and then in joint degree programs  those programs also allow independent  study in  in their discipline so you could  actually do more than the four  if you're doing a joint degree thank you  jennifer  one more question for you a question  came up  uh from julia was wondering that on our  website we have a list of sort of  approved formal  joint degree programs at stanford but  she's wondering why there might be some  there and not others so we have  an electrical engineering joint degree  but we don't have one say in mechanical  engineering  so this is a good time to mention to  folks that you can actually customize  a dual degree during your time at  stanford can you speak a little bit to  that process  sure so the joint degrees that are  currently approved have  um you know they've been formally  approved by the faculty senate at  stanford  and they are on the list because those  departments and programs had  an interest in joining up with the gsb  to offer a joint degree  but as christine mentioned if you are  interested in a graduate degree that is  not on that list so something like  mechanical engineering  you are welcome to apply to that it will  be an independent application process to  that department through their own  application process  and if admitted you would be considered  a dual degree so it's a little bit  different i'm not getting into the weeds  on it right now but  um you are able to do that and it's very  much  set up individually so we would just be  talking one-on-one about um  what those requirements would be and  what they mean for your mba  great thank you jennifer well we've  covered a lot of information today and i  know we're coming up on time  but of course one of i think the  greatest values in in time with alumni  is really getting to hear about your  personal experiences so as we're  preparing to wrap up today  i would love if you could each take a  moment to reflect on maybe one of your  favorite  extracurricular activities or  experiences you had  as a student at the gsb uh that was most  memorable or uh you know meaningful  to you  for me is talk the tradition of i don't  know  if any of you know but i'll explain  quickly talk is a tradition that every  in my class it was every monday two  students from the class volunteered  to give a story and most of the times  tend to be a personal story and it's  in 30 minutes you decide what you want  to tell your classmates  and in my case i suffer of the imposter  syndrome when i got to the gsb  i always wonder why am i the one to go  there  like and so like todd gave me the space  to give people  that story or what made me unique and it  was  amazing to have uh like 200 classmates  just interested on listening who you are  and then vice versa i also went almost  every monday to hear the stories of my  classmates and you get to meet that  behind  these amazing like talented individuals  that is these uh very human stories  that that makes them like amazing like  that's what i'm saying the people at the  gsb  i'm like always like i will be in the  classroom if  like what is behind that person and tag  gave me that ability  to to hear like i probably hear over 200  attack because  even at the end of the school year they  do like  a whole day old talk story so like i  definitely recommend you  if you get to go to the gsb to go to a  talk or give a talk  um for me i think um one of the most  memorable things was  actually having a chance to co-found  this this conference called the like  stanford climate business and innovation  conference  which i started with three other  classmates um and  it was just this amazing thing you know  we've talked about people a lot but just  the process of putting the conference  together like with this cohort of  like-minded students and  and the fact that it's actually the  conference is like continued on like it  still exists and sort of gets passed  down and so  there's kind of this like lineage now  that i've like gotten to know which is  terrific  um but it was just it was it was a lot  of fun it was like you know it was both  this  like intellectual convening around you  know a topic that we all really cared  about it was also kind of a party so it  was like the best of both worlds and  and then on top of that um just like the  the ability to  meet all these new people through the  event it's actually i know i said this  earlier but  i i still talk to people who i met like  in industry through that conference like  even two weeks ago someone like  referenced something i'd done at the  conference and i was like that's like in  2018  um but that was just a really memorable  sort of project and  and challenge for us to put on and has  meant a lot  yeah i'll second talk totally  transformational experience you won't  find anything like that anywhere else  um but other extracurriculars are great  uh  uh one of the biggest ones is the gsb  show  uh which i was very involved with uh  incredible  just coming together of uh you know dsb  students to put a lot of time into a  project together which is a big musical  uh at the end of the year uh that's  newly written every year and just one of  those fun  quirky things that everyone kind of puts  their hands together and has a lot of  fun doing it  so my best friends still uh the people i  talk to still  multiple times a week uh yeah we did  that together  and then one last thing i'll say some of  the most memorable experiences were on  the international experiences either the  official ones  or the unofficial ones both are  incredible  in different ways but you know through  the sort of  official ones getting to like learn and  travel with professors and and it's just  incredible experience i went to ethiopia  and tanzania  um and that was incredible um but also  did  you know student-led one uh to india and  never been to india  uh you know they they were from there  and sort of  showed us around and a really incredible  experience  you know trip of a lifetime uh and there  are a lot of experiences like that  at the gsb well thank you all so much  for  taking the time to share your  experiences with us today we really  appreciate it  jennifer as we wrap up today do you have  any  advice as one of the advisors to our  students  for students that are thinking about  pursuing a joint  or dual degree anything for them to  maybe walk away and keep in mind as they  are  considering applying uh when it comes to  how they should focus their time  and their academics well that's a tough  question  you're making me end it oh my gosh um i  think  my best advice is be thoughtful about  this  um it's an investment in time of course  but  it's really important to to always kind  of take a step back you know why are you  applying for the mba  ask yourself the same exact question why  are you applying for the joint degree  and really really think about what that  answer is what do you want to get out of  it  what skills are you hoping to learn what  content are you hoping to learn  how will this help you in your career  path and your ultimate goals after  you're done at the gsb  um that's the kind of broadest  best advice i can offer is to really  really think about this  um is this something you really want to  do is it something you feel you need to  do  um you know joint degrees are as i said  they take they're not it's not as simple  just add on a degree  um it's an investment in time it's an  investment of your brain waves all of  that stuff  and you know i just want joint degree  students to be  